{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_71","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/02-derivation-pipeline/epic.md | sed -n '120,170p;260,292p;360,390p'\"","aggregated_output":"   120\t1. Intake lands a message; `messages` queues the work its kind needs (Epic 01 behavior, extended with `tool_call_summary`)\n   121\t2. Drain dispatches to the messages handler\n   122\t3. Handler reads the message (for a tool call: joins its paired result by call id), calls the provider's semantic operation\n   123\t4. Handler stamps the outcome on tool-activity summaries from `isError` and result presence\n   124\t5. Smoothed form or summary lands `ready` with its content; provider failure past retry lands it `failed` with reason\n   125\t\n   126\t#### Acceptance Criteria\n   127\t\n   128\t- **AC-2.1**: A user prompt message's smoothing work, when run, lands a smoothed form in `ready` state, readable alongside the message.\n   129\t- **AC-2.2**: A tool-call message's summary work lands a summary naming the tool and describing its arguments, with an outcome field; a `tool_call` event in an intake batch queues a `tool_call_summary` work item the same way Epic 01 queues the other message-level kinds — an additive extension of the Epic 01 intake path that stays deterministic and local.\n   130\t- **AC-2.3**: A tool-result message's summary work lands a summarized abbreviation in `ready` state; the full result content remains untouched in the record.\n   131\t- **AC-2.4**: A tool-activity summary's outcome is stamped from the record: result present with `isError: false` → succeeded; `isError: true` → failed; no paired result → unknown. The provider's text never determines the outcome field.\n   132\t- **AC-2.5**: Message-level derivation reads only the message and its call-id-paired counterpart; no turn or chunk context is an input.\n   133\t- **AC-2.6**: A provider failure on a message-level item follows Flow 1 retry policy; exhaustion lands the form `failed` with the provider's reason, and the message itself remains fully readable.\n   134\t- **AC-2.7**: Message kinds with no derivable form (assistant text, thinking, runtime notes) queue no message-level work and carry no derivation state rows.\n   135\t- **AC-2.8**: A tool result arriving after its call's summary already landed with outcome `unknown` re-queues that summary at intake: the pair is the summary's source, and a source completing follows clear-and-regenerate like any source change. The check is deterministic and local (one indexed lookup by call id); the common case — call and result in one batch — never triggers it, because the summary runs after both landed.\n   136\t\n   137\t#### Test Conditions\n   138\t\n   139\t- **TC-2.1** (AC-2.1): Intake a prompt, drain → smoothed form `ready`, content is the double's deterministic output for that input.\n   140\t- **TC-2.2** (AC-2.2): Intake a `tool_call` event → batch result reports a `tool_call_summary` work item queued, intake result returned before any handler ran; drain → summary `ready`, names the tool, describes the arguments.\n   141\t- **TC-2.3** (AC-2.3): Intake a tool call and large result, drain → result summary `ready`; full content reads back byte-identical to intake.\n   142\t- **TC-2.4** (AC-2.4): Three intake variants — result ok, result `isError`, call with no result → outcomes succeeded, failed, unknown; double's text identical across all three, proving text doesn't drive outcome.\n   143\t- **TC-2.5** (AC-2.5): Double records its inputs → tool-call summary input contains the call and paired result only; no turn data.\n   144\t- **TC-2.6** (AC-2.6): Double exhausts retries on smoothing → form `failed` with reason; message read-back unaffected.\n   145\t- **TC-2.7** (AC-2.7): Intake assistant text and a runtime note, drain → no work items for them, no derivation state rows on read-back.\n   146\t- **TC-2.8** (AC-2.8): Intake a `tool_call` alone, drain (summary lands `unknown`), then intake its `tool_result` in a later batch → summary work re-queued at intake; drain → outcome now `succeeded`, one summary form, no duplicates. Control: call and result in one batch → no re-queue ever fires.\n   147\t\n   148\t---\n   149\t\n   150\t## Flow 3: Turn Composition and Chunk Formation\n   151\t\n   152\tWhen a turn closes, Epic 01 queues its derivation work. This flow runs it: the turn gets a smoothed rendering composed from its messages' already-derived forms, and a lower-band projection built from that rendering. The turn's tool activity appears in the rendering as composed accounts of what each run of calls did and how it ended, not as separate call listings. The closed turn then joins the open chunk; when accumulated size crosses the close policy, the chunk closes and queues its own summaries.\n   153\t\n   154\t1. Turn closes at intake (Epic 01); `turn_derivation` work queues\n   155\t2. Drain dispatches to the turns handler\n   156\t3. Handler composes the smoothed rendering from message-level forms, with tool runs composed into outcome-explicit accounts; where a form is `pending` or `failed`, the handler falls back to raw or truncated content and records the gap on the landed artifact\n   157\t4. Handler builds the lower-band projection from the rendering\n   158\t5. Turn joins the open chunk; if accumulated projected size crosses the close threshold, the chunk closes and queues detailed and brief summary work\n   159\t6. Chunk summary work runs: detailed and brief summaries land with state\n   160\t\n   161\t#### Acceptance Criteria\n   162\t\n   163\t- **AC-3.1**: A closed turn's derivation work lands a smoothed rendering and a lower-band projection, each carrying its own state.\n   164\t- **AC-3.2**: The rendering composes message-level forms where they are `ready`; where a form is `pending` or `failed`, the rendering uses the message's raw or truncated content and still lands `ready`, recording each fallback as a dependency gap on the artifact — message-form gaps degrade the rendering's inputs, they do not fail the turn.\n   165\t- **AC-3.3**: A dependency later becoming `ready` does not silently change artifacts that landed with a gap: they stay `ready`, their gap records stand, and the report surfaces them; rebuilding a gapped artifact is an explicit re-queue through its owning surface (Flow 4), never an automatic cascade.\n   166\t- **AC-3.4**: Tool activity in a rendering appears as composed accounts of runs, each stating its outcome; a run containing a state-changing call never loses its outcome in composition.\n   167\t- **AC-3.5**: A turn whose lower-band projection succeeded joins the open chunk; placement is recorded with the turn and readable through `turns`.\n   168\t- **AC-3.6**: A chunk closes when the accumulated projected size of its turns plus the incoming turn's crosses the close target; the incoming turn starts the next chunk when placement would cross it, per the policy's accumulation rule.\n   169\t- **AC-3.7**: A single turn whose projection alone meets or exceeds the close maximum forms its own chunk immediately.\n   170\t- **AC-3.8**: Chunk close queues detailed and brief summary work as two work items with independent retry; both land as chunk-level derived forms with independent states. The detailed summary preserves tool-activity receipts (what changed, outcome); the brief summary preserves outcomes only.\n   260\t\n   261\tAs with edit: deletes never mutate an existing generated thread-view — visibility arrives at the next compact/rebuild.\n   262\t\n   263\t#### Acceptance Criteria\n   264\t\n   265\t- **AC-6.1**: A deleted message no longer appears in message reads or its turn's membership; its source events remain in the event log, readable through the Epic 01 event read-back.\n   266\t- **AC-6.2**: A deleted message's own derived forms drop with it; its turn's rendering and projection clear and re-queue; the containing chunk's summaries clear and re-queue; nothing else changes.\n   267\t- **AC-6.3**: Deleting a message that initiates a turn is refused with an error naming the turn and the turn-delete path; nothing changes.\n   268\t- **AC-6.4**: Deleting a turn through `turns` drops the turn and all its messages from the readable record and from chunk membership; source events remain.\n   269\t- **AC-6.5**: A deleted turn's chunk re-derives its summaries from the remaining turns; chunk boundaries do not move; no other chunk's membership or derivations change.\n   270\t- **AC-6.6**: Deleting every turn in a chunk leaves an empty chunk that contributes nothing to reads; its summary forms are dropped, not failed.\n   271\t- **AC-6.7**: Deletes against the open turn, missing ids, or already-deleted targets are refused with stable errors; refusal changes nothing; delete of the same id twice is a refusal, not a silent success.\n   272\t- **AC-6.8**: Message delete and turn delete are available on the SDK and as CLI commands with parity.\n   273\t\n   274\t#### Test Conditions\n   275\t\n   276\t- **TC-6.1** (AC-6.1): Delete a tool-result message → message reads and turn membership exclude it; event read-back still returns its events.\n   277\t- **TC-6.2** (AC-6.2): Delete a message in a two-chunk thread → its forms gone; turn forms and chunk-1 summaries `pending` and queued; chunk 2 untouched.\n   278\t- **TC-6.3** (AC-6.3): Delete a turn-initiating prompt → refused; error names the turn id and turns-delete; full read-back unchanged.\n   279\t- **TC-6.4** (AC-6.4): Delete a three-message turn via `turns` → turn and messages gone from reads and chunk membership; events all present.\n   280\t- **TC-6.5** (AC-6.5): Two-chunk thread, delete a turn from chunk 1, drain → chunk 1 summaries rebuilt from remaining turns (double input proves source set); chunk 2 forms untouched; boundaries identical.\n   281\t- **TC-6.6** (AC-6.6): Delete both turns of a chunk → chunk empty, summary forms absent, reads skip it without error.\n   282\t- **TC-6.7** (AC-6.7): Delete open-turn message; delete a bogus id; delete the same message twice → three refusals with stable codes; record identical after each.\n   283\t- **TC-6.8** (AC-6.8): Same delete via SDK and CLI on twin fixtures → identical results and read-back.\n   284\t\n   285\t---\n   286\t\n   287\t## Data Contracts\n   288\t\n   289\t### Work Item (extended from Epic 01)\n   290\t\n   291\tEpic 01 ships `WorkItemRecord` with `kind: \"prompt_smoothing\" | \"tool_result_summary\" | \"turn_derivation\"`. This epic extends the kind set additively:\n   292\t\n   360\t| `turn_open` | caller_error | mutation against the open turn |\n   361\t| `message_initiates_turn` | caller_error | message delete targeting a turn-initiating prompt |\n   362\t| `not_found` | caller_error | mutation or re-queue against a missing id (Epic 01 code reused where it exists) |\n   363\t| `unknown_work_kind` | state_corruption | drain encountering an unregistered kind |\n   364\t| `provider_failure` | system_error | provider failure surfaced as a form's failed reason |\n   365\t| `source_damaged` | state_corruption | handler finding corrupt source; the form's blocked reason |\n   366\t\n   367\t### Cascade Scope\n   368\t\n   369\t| Mutation | Drops (source gone) | Clears and re-queues |\n   370\t|----------|--------------------|--------------------|\n   371\t| edit message | — | message's own forms; turn rendering + projection; containing chunk's detailed + brief |\n   372\t| delete message | message's own forms | turn rendering + projection; containing chunk's detailed + brief |\n   373\t| delete turn | turn's forms, rendering, projection; its messages' forms | containing chunk's detailed + brief |\n   374\t\n   375\tBounding rule: a turn lives in exactly one chunk; chunk summaries derive only from their own members; therefore no cascade crosses a chunk boundary, and boundaries never move.\n   376\t\n   377\t---\n   378\t\n   379\t## Dependencies\n   380\t\n   381\tTechnical:\n   382\t- Epic 01 complete: intake, projection, turn state machine, work-item queueing, read-back surfaces\n   383\t- Epic 01's `WorkItemRecord` contract and `tech-utils/work-queue` placement\n   384\t- Epic 01's error result classes (`caller_error`, `state_corruption`, `system_error`) and code conventions\n   385\t\n   386\tProcess:\n   387\t- Chunk close policy values (target, max) are config with defaults; tech design pins the defaults\n   388\t\n   389\t---\n   390\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
