{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5","id":"msg_017TBSWp4xa6sb8nF89sRZ83","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The review is complete — I've read the epic, tech design, test plan, impl log, and all production source for the epic (scheduler, work-queue, shared layer, both domains, providers, CLI), run the epic gate (`pnpm run verify-all`: 29 files, 231 tests, exit 0), confirmed all 46 TC ids present in test titles, swept for non-real production paths, and checked the boundary script. Verdict: pass, with non-blocking findings only.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"outcome\": \"pass\",\n  \"crossStoryFindings\": [\n    \"The story chain composes into one coherent mechanism with no reimplementation across stories: enqueue (Story 0) is the single creation point for derived_form rows, completion is UPDATE-only and source-version-checked in the queue util (Story 1), handlers return form writes as data (Stories 2-3), report/requeue join the same live work_item rows (Story 4), and mutations reuse the identical enqueue/supersede/version machinery (Stories 5-6).\",\n    \"DD-8's one-cascade rule held across stories: messages/internal/cascade.ts serves edit, message delete, and turn delete; turns.deleteTurn reaches it only through the messages domain's public surface (applyTurnDeleteCascade), and the boundary script confirms no cross-domain internal imports anywhere.\",\n    \"The deleted-read filter rule is applied consistently at every read site introduced across Stories 3-6: readMessages, readMutableMessage, readMemberMessages, readMemberProjections, readChunkRows, readTurns (both membership and turn rows), and readMutableTurn all append deleted_at IS NULL; the two sanctioned exceptions (event read-back and the handlers' readMessageSource) are deliberate and documented in place.\",\n    \"Drain report dispositions match DD-1's five-disposition contract exactly across the scheduler, queue util, and mutation results: done/stale_discarded/failed_terminal appear only in drain reports, superseded appears only on MutationResult, and terminal rows are deleted in the same transaction that records their outcome.\",\n    \"Work-item identity (w-<sourceId>-<kind>-v<sourceVersion>) is honored consistently by intake queueing, the AC-2.8 late-result requeue, both owners' repair requeues, and the mutation cascade's replacement enqueues, so the DD-3 stale-discard truth table holds at every write path I traced.\",\n    \"All implementation-time deviations are recorded and ruled in the team impl log (5 in Story 0, 7 in Story 1, 5 in Story 2, 3 in Story 3, the rejected-then-fixed AC-4.7 set in Story 4, 4 in Story 5, 2 in Story 6); I found no unrecorded drift between the code and the rulings.\"\n  ],\n  \"architectureFindings\": [\n    \"Module placement matches the tech design with two approved, recorded renames: turn handlers live in turns/internal/derive.ts rather than handlers.ts (Story 3 ruling), and the scheduler poke/touch seams live in shared/context.ts rather than the scheduler module to avoid an import cycle (Story 0 ruling, DD-5 semantics preserved).\",\n    \"Migration v5 implements the tech-design schema faithfully, including the F-02 backfill of pending form rows for pre-v5 queued items, plus the approved deviation that idx_work_item_queue omits rowid (SQLite forbids rowid in index definitions; claimNext's ORDER BY rowid preserves head-first semantics).\",\n    \"claimNext is the single atomic UPDATE under BEGIN IMMEDIATE specified in Part 3b, with the head re-read classifying empty/in_flight/waiting and the reclaim CASE incrementing attempts; the never-skip-ahead rule is structural, not procedural.\",\n    \"Provider calls never run inside a transaction: handlers receive openDb() for short reads, return form writes as data, and the queue util owns the completion transaction; the onApplied hook makes chunk placement and summary enqueues ride the same completion commit, so a crash leaves either a placed turn with queued summaries or nothing.\",\n    \"The chunk close policy implements DD-9's accumulation rule (close at accumulated + incoming >= target with the incoming turn starting the next chunk, single turn >= max closes its own chunk, empty chunks always accept) over stored projected-token values only, which is what makes TC-3.9 replay determinism hold.\",\n    \"The tech design said the boundary script would gain a scheduler allowed-import line; none was added because none is needed — scheduler.ts imports only domain public indexes, which the existing rules permit. The protection the design wanted exists; the specific mechanism was moot.\",\n    \"Minor drift: createSdk's injected clock governs drain and scheduler timestamps, but the mutation and requeue operations construct their own wall clock (runInTransaction(db, () => new Date(), ...)) for deleted_at and queuedAt stamps, so deterministic time injection does not extend to the mutation surfaces (see non-blocking finding E02-NB-001).\"\n  ],\n  \"epicCoverageAssessment\": [\n    \"Epic gate pnpm run verify-all passed from packages/lhc with exit 0: 29 test files, 231 tests, including the LHC_PROCESS_SUITE=1 spawned-process suite covering the architecture-risk fixtures (TC-1.3 SIGKILL restart survival, TC-1.4 cross-process claim exclusion).\",\n    \"All 46 TC ids from the epic appear verbatim in test titles across the suites (TC-1.1 through TC-6.8, plus Epic 01's TC-2.9 kind-gate amendment), matching the test plan's file mapping; the suite grew from Epic 01's 118 tests to 231, exceeding the plan's 60-70 estimate.\",\n    \"All 47 ACs trace to implemented behavior I read directly: queue execution and both host modes (scheduler.ts, work-queue), message derivation with mechanically stamped outcomes (handlers.ts, outcome.ts), turn composition with gap recording and deterministic chunking (derive.ts, compose.ts, chunks.ts), report/repair with blocked refusal and requeue idempotency (both owners' index.ts and forms.ts), and the three mutations with one-transaction cascade, supersede, and stale-write fencing (cascade.ts, store.ts).\",\n    \"The four architecture-risk tests named by the test plan (TC-1.3, TC-1.4, TC-5.4, TC-3.9) are all present and green in the gate run.\",\n    \"CLI parity is real: every SDK surface (drain, report, requeue, edit, delete message, delete turn) has a CLI command that calls the same domain operation with the same validation and result JSON, and the CLI drain assembles a manual-mode SDK through createSdk rather than an in-process shortcut.\"\n  ],\n  \"productionPathFindings\": [\n    \"No fake adapters, stubs, placeholders, or TODO/FIXME markers exist anywhere in src/; every handler, mutation, and read path is real execution against real SQLite state.\",\n    \"The deterministic provider ships in production source (src/providers/deterministic.ts) and is registered in the production named-provider registry. This is explicitly sanctioned by DD-11 and an approved Story 1 ruling: it is selectable only by explicit name (--provider deterministic / LHC_PROVIDER=deterministic), is never a default, and exists so spawned-CLI tests exercise the production resolution seam. A drain without a resolvable provider fails with provider_not_configured naming the flag and env var — fail-closed, never a silent no-op.\",\n    \"The deterministic provider is currently the ONLY registered named provider, so a production CLI drain can only produce marked deterministic artifacts today. This is consistent with the epic's explicit scope exclusion (real provider adapters are out of scope; this epic ships the seam and the double), but operators should understand real inference requires a future adapter registration.\",\n    \"Test seams compiled into production modules: setIntakeWalkHook and setIntakeClock in intake-stream/internal/pipeline.ts (Epic 01 carryover, set only through test fixtures, explicit-argument-wins), and Scheduler.testPassCount plus the passes counter in scheduler.ts (documented test-only observability, not API). All are inert in production assembly and documented in place; none fakes an execution path.\",\n    \"Background-mode scheduling is gated fail-closed on hasAnyHandler so an empty handler map can never turn queued rows into failed_terminal; in the shipped assembly both domains register real handlers, so the gate is always open in production.\"\n  ],\n  \"blockingFindings\": [],\n  \"nonBlockingFindings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E02-NB-001\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"title\": \"Mutation and requeue operations bypass the SDK-injected clock\",\n      \"evidence\": \"messages.edit, messages.deleteMessage, messages.requeue, turns.requeue, and turns.deleteTurn all call runInTransaction(db, () => new Date(), threadId, ...) — deleted_at stamps and requeued items' queuedAt use wall time regardless of SdkConfig.clock, which governs only drain/scheduler paths (and intake has its own setIntakeClock seam). Deterministic time injection therefore does not extend to the mutation surfaces.\",\n      \"affectedFiles\": [\n        \"packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/index.ts\",\n        \"packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/index.ts\"\n      ],\n      \"requirementIds\": [\"DD-7\", \"AC-5.1\", \"AC-6.1\"],\n      \"recommendedFixScope\": \"quick-fix\",\n      \"blocking\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E02-NB-002\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"title\": \"Process-global scheduler seams not cleared by a later manual-mode SDK\",\n      \"evidence\": \"createSdk installs setSchedulerPoke/setThreadTouch only in background mode and a later background SDK supersedes an earlier one (documented), but a manual-mode SDK constructed after a background one leaves the previous background scheduler installed — enqueues through the manual SDK would still poke the stale scheduler and drain with the old provider/config. Low real-world impact under assumption A3 (one host per process), but the background-then-manual sequence is unhandled.\",\n      \"affectedFiles\": [\n        \"packages/lhc/src/sdk.ts\",\n        \"packages/lhc/src/shared/context.ts\",\n        \"packages/lhc/src/scheduler.ts\"\n      ],\n      \"requirementIds\": [\"DD-4\", \"DD-5\", \"AC-1.7\"],\n      \"recommendedFixScope\": \"quick-fix\",\n      \"blocking\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E02-NB-003\",\n      \"severity\": \"observation\",\n      \"title\": \"Deterministic provider is the only registered production provider\",\n      \"evidence\": \"src/providers/registry.ts registers exactly one name ('deterministic'). Real adapters are explicitly out of epic scope and DD-11 sanctions the double's registry placement with no default, so this is by design — but until a real adapter lands, lhc work drain in production can only produce marked deterministic artifacts, selected by explicit operator opt-in.\",\n      \"affectedFiles\": [\n        \"packages/lhc/src/providers/registry.ts\",\n        \"packages/lhc/src/providers/deterministic.ts\"\n      ],\n      \"requirementIds\": [\"DD-11\"],\n      \"recommendedFixScope\": \"human-ruling\",\n      \"blocking\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E02-NB-004\",\n      \"severity\": \"observation\",\n      \"title\": \"Thrown handler errors are converted to retryable attempts\",\n      \"evidence\": \"drainOpenDb catches a throwing handler and routes it through failAttempt as { retryable: true }, consuming retry budget before landing failed. The error contract says bugs throw and are not contract outcomes; this deliberately softens that to protect the queue from wedging (documented in scheduler.ts). The trade-off is reasonable but means a deterministic handler bug is retried twice before surfacing.\",\n      \"affectedFiles\": [\"packages/lhc/src/scheduler.ts\"],\n      \"requirementIds\": [\"AC-1.9\"],\n      \"recommendedFixScope\": \"human-ruling\",\n      \"blocking\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E02-NB-005\",\n      \"severity\": \"observation\",\n      \"title\": \"Inconsistent attempts accounting between terminal paths\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The blocked-source terminal path stamps attempts: item.attempts + 1 while the unknown-kind terminal path stamps attempts: item.attempts (scheduler.ts lines 119-125 vs 158-165). Cosmetic: the value only feeds the drain report and the form's failure metadata, and unknown-kind items never ran a handler, but the asymmetry is undocumented.\",\n      \"affectedFiles\": [\"packages/lhc/src/scheduler.ts\"],\n      \"requirementIds\": [\"AC-1.8\", \"AC-1.9\"],\n      \"recommendedFixScope\": \"quick-fix\",\n      \"blocking\": false\n    }\n  ],\n  \"unresolvedItems\": [],\n  \"gateResult\": \"pass\"\n}\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2683,"cache_read_input_tokens":186927,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2683},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1746f4bf-569a-4870-8bca-7c80d2cc25af","uuid":"83327b15-85fc-4542-9e16-6f371fac95c9","request_id":"req_011Cbw8FEAAZeEfHFhMW5FgM"}
