{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":353220,"duration_api_ms":330113,"ttft_ms":15704,"ttft_stream_ms":4766,"time_to_request_ms":19,"num_turns":15,"result":"All verification is complete. Both blocking findings are confirmed in code, all of reviewer-1's non-blocking findings check out, and the epic gate passes (exit 0, 29 files / 231 tests). Producing the canonical review.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"outcome\": \"block\",\n  \"reviewerLabels\": [\"epic-reviewer-1\", \"epic-reviewer-2\"],\n  \"reconciliationSummary\": \"Two reviewers split: epic-reviewer-1 (claude-code) returned pass with five non-blocking findings; epic-reviewer-2 (codex) returned block with two majors, both verified dynamically. Independent re-verification against the code confirms both blockers, so the conservative rule holds and neither is downgraded. (1) Mode isolation: both reviewers found the same defect — reviewer-1 as minor E02-NB-002, reviewer-2 as blocking EPIC-02-BLOCK-001. Code confirms it: sdk.ts:254-257 installs the process-global scheduler poke and thread-touch seams only in background mode and manual mode 'leaves the slots alone'; shared/context.ts:40-48 holds module-global slots that nothing clears; work-queue enqueue (index.ts:136) unconditionally registers onCommit(() => fireSchedulerPoke(threadId)). A manual SDK constructed after a background SDK therefore drains automatically through the stale background scheduler, violating AC-1.7's manual-mode contract. The sdk.ts comment explicitly contemplates multi-SDK-per-process (background supersedes background), so the background-then-manual sequence is inside the design's contemplated space, not an unsupported configuration. The two findings are merged into one blocking finding; reviewer-1's minor classification is the material disagreement, resolved conservatively toward the dynamically verified blocker. (2) Deleted-pair outcomes: only reviewer-2 found EPIC-02-BLOCK-002, and every step of its claimed code path verifies: findPairedBlock (messages/internal/forms.ts:163-178) joins message with no deleted_at IS NULL filter — an unsanctioned exception to tech-design Part 3b's deleted-read filter rule, which names exactly two exceptions, neither of them the pairing read; toolCallSummaryHandler (handlers.ts:98-105) stamps outcome from that unfiltered read, so a tool_call_summary requeued after its paired result is deleted rebuilds outcome 'succeeded' from a record no longer in the readable record (against AC-2.4's no-paired-result→unknown and AC-6.1); cascadeMessageDelete (cascade.ts:209-215) drops the deleted message's own forms and clears upward only, never touching the paired counterpart's summary; and compose.ts:150-153 prefers the ready summary's stale metadata over its own mechanical outcomeFromRecord fallback, so the delete cascade's own rendering rebuild republishes the stale outcome. This partially invalidates reviewer-1's cross-story claim that the deleted-read filter is applied at every read site with only sanctioned exceptions. There is genuine spec tension behind the cascade half — AC-6.2's 'nothing else changes' excludes the counterpart summary from the cascade while AC-2.8 establishes the pair as the summary's source — carried as an unresolved item needing a human ruling; the missing read filter is an unambiguous defect regardless of that ruling. Reviewer-1's remaining non-blocking findings (E02-NB-001 wall-clock in mutations confirmed at five runInTransaction(db, () => new Date()) sites; E02-NB-003 deterministic-only registry confirmed in providers/registry.ts; E02-NB-004 thrown-handler-to-retryable confirmed at scheduler.ts:139-145; E02-NB-005 attempts asymmetry confirmed at scheduler.ts:119-125 vs 158-165) are all supported and retained. Epic gate re-run independently: pnpm run verify-all exit 0 (build, typecheck, lint, boundaries, 29 test files, 231 tests) — a passing gate does not offset the confirmed blockers, since the missing coverage (cross-SDK mode isolation, delete of a paired tool result) is exactly why the suite stays green.\",\n  \"crossStoryFindings\": [\n    \"Confirmed: manual/background mode isolation does not survive SDK sequencing within one process. Background-mode construction installs process-global scheduler poke and thread-touch seams (sdk.ts:254-257, shared/context.ts:40-48) that a later manual-mode SDK never clears, and every enqueue unconditionally fires the global poke post-commit (work-queue/index.ts:136), so work queued through a manual SDK drains automatically through the leftover background scheduler — with that scheduler's provider and config — without any drain call.\",\n    \"Confirmed: the deletion flow does not compose with paired tool-call/tool-result derivation. The pairing read crosses the deleted-read filter discipline (findPairedBlock has no deleted_at filter), the delete cascade never clears the paired counterpart's tool_call_summary even though AC-2.8 makes the pair the summary's source, and turn-rendering composition (compose.ts:150-153) trusts the stale ready summary's metadata over its own record-derived fallback — so a deleted tool result's outcome survives into rebuilt renderings and even into an explicit post-delete requeue of the summary.\",\n    \"The story chain otherwise composes into one coherent mechanism: enqueue is the single creation point for derived_form rows, completion is UPDATE-only and source-version-checked, handlers return form writes as data, report/requeue join live work_item rows, and all three mutations reuse the identical enqueue/supersede/version machinery through the one shared cascade module (DD-8), with turns.deleteTurn reaching it only via the messages domain's public surface.\",\n    \"The deleted-read filter rule is applied at the membership and read-back sites introduced across Stories 3-6 (message reads, member projections, chunk rows, turn reads), but reviewer-1's claim that every read site complies with only sanctioned exceptions is corrected by the confirmed blocker: the call-id pairing read is an unfiltered read of message content that the tech design's two-exception list does not sanction.\",\n    \"Work-item identity (w-<sourceId>-<kind>-v<sourceVersion>), the DD-1 five-disposition contract, and the DD-3 stale-discard truth table hold consistently across intake queueing, drain, repair requeues, and the mutation cascade's replacement enqueues.\"\n  ],\n  \"architectureFindings\": [\n    \"Confirmed drift from the architecture contract: mode selection is declared per SDK instance (DD-4: hosts see mode at construction) but enforced through process-global mutable hooks, so the mode contract is only as strong as the last background construction in the process; manual mode's AC-1.7 guarantee is not enforceable by the manual instance itself. The fix needs a small design decision — clear the slots on manual construction, scope the seams per SDK instance, or refuse multi-SDK construction — though all variants are mechanically small.\",\n    \"Confirmed: the deleted-read filter discipline (tech design Part 3b) misses the tool pairing read path, and the mutation cascade's scope (derived structurally from the message→turn→chunk walk in cascade.ts) cannot reach the paired counterpart's summary, which the derivation architecture (AC-2.8, outcome stamping from the pair) makes a real source dependency. The epic's own cascade-scope table ('nothing else changes') conflicts with the pair-as-source model AC-2.8 introduced — a spec-level gap, not just an implementation one.\",\n    \"Module placement otherwise matches the tech design with two approved, recorded renames (turn handlers in turns/internal/derive.ts; scheduler seams in shared/context.ts to avoid an import cycle, DD-5 semantics preserved); migration v5 implements the schema faithfully including the F-02 backfill; claimNext is the single atomic UPDATE under BEGIN IMMEDIATE with structural never-skip-ahead; provider calls never run inside a transaction; the DD-9 chunk close policy reads only stored projected-token values, which is what makes TC-3.9 replay determinism hold.\",\n    \"Minor drift retained from reviewer-1: createSdk's injected clock governs drain and scheduler timestamps, but mutation and requeue operations construct their own wall clock (five runInTransaction(db, () => new Date(), ...) sites in messages/index.ts and turns/index.ts), so deterministic time injection does not extend to the mutation surfaces (E02-NB-001).\"\n  ],\n  \"epicCoverageAssessment\": [\n    \"Epic gate independently re-run for this canonical review: pnpm run verify-all from packages/lhc exits 0 — build, typecheck, lint (75 files), boundary check, and 29 test files / 231 tests all green, including the LHC_PROCESS_SUITE spawned-process fixtures (TC-1.3 SIGKILL restart survival, TC-1.4 cross-process claim exclusion).\",\n    \"All 46 TC ids appear verbatim in test titles and all 47 ACs trace to implemented behavior per reviewer-1's file-level audit; the four architecture-risk tests named by the test plan (TC-1.3, TC-1.4, TC-5.4, TC-3.9) are present and green; CLI parity surfaces call the same domain operations with the same validation and result JSON.\",\n    \"Two coverage gaps correspond exactly to the confirmed blockers: no test constructs a background SDK followed by a manual SDK in one process (the AC-1.7 isolation case), and no test deletes or edits a tool result whose paired call summary already landed (the AC-2.4/AC-6.2 pairing case). Both blockers' fix paths must add these tests.\"\n  ],\n  \"productionPathFindings\": [\n    \"No fake adapters, stubs, placeholders, or silent provider defaults: CLI drain requires --provider or LHC_PROVIDER and fails closed with provider_not_configured; the deterministic provider is explicit-only per DD-11 and is currently the sole registered named provider (real adapters are an explicit epic scope exclusion — operators need a future adapter registration for real inference; E02-NB-003).\",\n    \"Material production-path issue confirmed: in any process that ever constructed a background SDK, a subsequently constructed manual SDK silently behaves as background — its enqueues drain through the stale scheduler with the prior SDK's provider and config (EPIC-02-BLOCK-001).\",\n    \"Material production-path issue confirmed: the tool pairing read returns deleted messages, so production drains and explicit requeues can land ready forms whose mechanically stamped outcomes derive from records outside the readable record (EPIC-02-BLOCK-002).\",\n    \"Test seams compiled into production modules (intake walk hook/clock, scheduler testPassCount) are inert in production assembly and documented in place; background scheduling is gated fail-closed on hasAnyHandler; thrown handler errors are deliberately softened to retryable attempts to keep the queue from wedging (scheduler.ts:139-145, E02-NB-004).\"\n  ],\n  \"blockingFindings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"EPIC-02-BLOCK-001\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"title\": \"Manual SDK mode can auto-drain after background SDK construction in the same process\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Confirmed in code (and dynamically by epic-reviewer-2; reviewer-1 independently found the same defect as E02-NB-002). createSdk installs the process-wide seams only for background mode and manual mode leaves them installed (packages/lhc/src/sdk.ts:254-257: installSchedulerPoke / setThreadTouch under `if (resolved.mode === \\\"background\\\")`, with the comment documenting only background-supersedes-background); the slots are module globals nothing else clears (packages/lhc/src/shared/context.ts:40-48); enqueue unconditionally registers ctx.onCommit(() => fireSchedulerPoke(ctx.threadId)) (packages/lhc/src/tech-utils/work-queue/index.ts:136); the background scheduler responds to the poke by draining (packages/lhc/src/scheduler.ts:315-319). Result: after `createSdk({mode:\\\"background\\\"})` then `createSdk({mode:\\\"manual\\\"})`, work queued via the manual SDK runs without any drain call, through the stale scheduler's provider and config — violating AC-1.7 ('queued work accumulates durably and does not run until the drain operation is invoked'). Reviewer-2 reproduced it end-to-end: w-m1-prompt_smoothing-v1 queued via the manual SDK drained with no manual drain invocation. Retained blocking per the conservative rule: the code confirms rather than disproves the blocker; assumption A3 narrows real-world exposure but the design itself contemplates multiple SDK constructions per process.\",\n      \"affectedFiles\": [\n        \"packages/lhc/src/sdk.ts\",\n        \"packages/lhc/src/shared/context.ts\",\n        \"packages/lhc/src/tech-utils/work-queue/index.ts\",\n        \"packages/lhc/src/scheduler.ts\"\n      ],\n      \"requirementIds\": [\"AC-1.7\", \"DD-4\", \"DD-5\", \"tech-design.md Flow 1\"],\n      \"recommendedFixScope\": \"quick-fix\",\n      \"blocking\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"EPIC-02-BLOCK-002\",\n      \"severity\": \"major\",\n      \"title\": \"Deleted tool results still determine tool-call outcomes through the unfiltered pairing read and an uncascaded counterpart summary\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Confirmed in code at every step of reviewer-2's claimed path. (1) findPairedBlock joins message with no deleted_at IS NULL (packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/internal/forms.ts:163-178) — an unsanctioned exception to tech design Part 3b's deleted-read filter rule, which names exactly two exceptions (event read-back, mutation validation's filtered existence check), not the pairing read. (2) toolCallSummaryHandler stamps the summary's outcome from that read (packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/internal/handlers.ts:98-105), so an explicit requeue of tool_call_summary after the paired tool_result is deleted rebuilds outcome 'succeeded' from a record no longer in the readable record — against AC-2.4's mechanical rule (no paired result → unknown) read with AC-6.1 (a deleted message no longer appears in reads). (3) cascadeMessageDelete drops the deleted message's own forms and clears only upward (turn, chunk) — the paired call's summary on a different message is never cleared (packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/internal/cascade.ts:209-215). (4) Composition prefers the ready summary's stale metadata over its own record-derived outcome fallback (packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/internal/compose.ts:150-153), so the delete cascade's own turn-rendering rebuild republishes the stale 'succeeded' while the rendering's membership correctly excludes the deleted result. The read-filter half is an unambiguous defect; the cascade half sits on a spec conflict between AC-6.2's 'nothing else changes' and AC-2.8's pair-as-source rule, carried as an unresolved item for a ruling.\",\n      \"affectedFiles\": [\n        \"packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/internal/forms.ts\",\n        \"packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/internal/handlers.ts\",\n        \"packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/internal/cascade.ts\",\n        \"packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/internal/compose.ts\"\n      ],\n      \"requirementIds\": [\"AC-2.4\", \"AC-2.8\", \"AC-6.1\", \"AC-6.2\", \"tech-design.md Part 3b deleted-read filter rule\"],\n      \"recommendedFixScope\": \"fresh-fix-path\",\n      \"blocking\": true\n    }\n  ],\n  \"nonBlockingFindings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E02-NB-001\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"title\": \"Mutation and requeue operations bypass the SDK-injected clock\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Confirmed: five sites call runInTransaction(db, () => new Date(), threadId, ...) — messages/index.ts:331, 421, 503 and turns/index.ts:356, 433 — so deleted_at stamps and requeued items' queuedAt use wall time regardless of SdkConfig.clock, which governs only drain/scheduler paths. Deterministic time injection does not extend to the mutation surfaces.\",\n      \"affectedFiles\": [\n        \"packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/index.ts\",\n        \"packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/index.ts\"\n      ],\n      \"requirementIds\": [\"DD-7\", \"AC-5.1\", \"AC-6.1\"],\n      \"recommendedFixScope\": \"quick-fix\",\n      \"blocking\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E02-NB-003\",\n      \"severity\": \"observation\",\n      \"title\": \"Deterministic provider is the only registered production provider\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Confirmed: src/providers/registry.ts registers exactly one name ('deterministic'). Real adapters are explicitly out of epic scope and DD-11 sanctions the double's registry placement with no default, so this is by design — but until a real adapter lands, lhc work drain in production can only produce marked deterministic artifacts, selected by explicit operator opt-in.\",\n      \"affectedFiles\": [\n        \"packages/lhc/src/providers/registry.ts\",\n        \"packages/lhc/src/providers/deterministic.ts\"\n      ],\n      \"requirementIds\": [\"DD-11\"],\n      \"recommendedFixScope\": \"human-ruling\",\n      \"blocking\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E02-NB-004\",\n      \"severity\": \"observation\",\n      \"title\": \"Thrown handler errors are converted to retryable attempts\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Confirmed at scheduler.ts:139-145: drainOpenDb catches a throwing handler and routes it through failAttempt as { retryable: true }, consuming retry budget before landing failed. The error contract says bugs throw and are not contract outcomes; this deliberately softens that to protect the queue from wedging (documented in place). Reasonable trade-off, but a deterministic handler bug is retried twice before surfacing.\",\n      \"affectedFiles\": [\"packages/lhc/src/scheduler.ts\"],\n      \"requirementIds\": [\"AC-1.9\"],\n      \"recommendedFixScope\": \"human-ruling\",\n      \"blocking\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E02-NB-005\",\n      \"severity\": \"observation\",\n      \"title\": \"Inconsistent attempts accounting between terminal paths\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Confirmed: the unknown-kind terminal path stamps attempts: item.attempts (scheduler.ts:119-125) while the blocked-source terminal path stamps attempts: item.attempts + 1 (scheduler.ts:159-165). Cosmetic — the value only feeds the drain report and the form's failure metadata, and unknown-kind items never ran a handler — but the asymmetry is undocumented.\",\n      \"affectedFiles\": [\"packages/lhc/src/scheduler.ts\"],\n      \"requirementIds\": [\"AC-1.8\", \"AC-1.9\"],\n      \"recommendedFixScope\": \"quick-fix\",\n      \"blocking\": false\n    }\n  ],\n  \"unresolvedItems\": [\n    \"Cascade contract ruling needed for paired tool-call/tool-result dependencies: AC-6.2's exhaustive cascade scope ('nothing else changes') conflicts with AC-2.8's pair-as-source model. Decide whether deleting (or editing) one half of a call/result pair must clear and requeue the counterpart's summary, and update the epic's cascade-scope table either way. The findPairedBlock deleted-read filter fix is unambiguous and should not wait on this ruling.\",\n    \"Design ruling needed on the mode-isolation fix shape for EPIC-02-BLOCK-001: clear the process-global seams on manual-mode construction, scope the poke/touch seams per SDK instance, or refuse multi-SDK construction per process — all small, but the choice affects the documented multi-SDK semantics in sdk.ts.\"\n  ],\n  \"gateResult\": \"pass\"\n}\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"966b484c-2144-4e17-9e11-a73523d7f0c4","total_cost_usd":3.77675,"usage":{"input_tokens":3311,"cache_creation_input_tokens":89790,"cache_read_input_tokens":783839,"output_tokens":23152,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":89790,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":10359,"cache_read_input_tokens":105063,"cache_creation_input_tokens":545,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":545},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":6311,"outputTokens":18,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.006401,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-fable-5":{"inputTokens":3311,"outputTokens":23152,"cacheReadInputTokens":783839,"cacheCreationInputTokens":89790,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":3.7703489999999995,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"5d4660a5-6669-474b-bbcf-acc40d5a55e2"}
